---
id: form-composition
title: Form Composition
---
NIFT_TANSTACK_DOCUMENT_V1
{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A common criticism of TanStack Form is its verbosity out-of-the-box. While this "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"can"}]},{"type":"text","value":" be useful for educational purposes - helping enforce understanding our APIs - it's not ideal in production use cases."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"As a result, while "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form.Field"},{"type":"text","value":" enables the most powerful and flexible usage of TanStack Form, we provide APIs that wrap it and make your application code less verbose."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"custom-form-hooks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Custom Form Hooks"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The most powerful way to compose forms is to create custom form hooks. This allows you to create a form hook that is tailored to your application's needs, including pre-bound custom UI components and more."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"At it's most basic, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createFormHook"},{"type":"text","value":" is a function that takes a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fieldContext"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"formContext"},{"type":"text","value":" and returns a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useAppForm"},{"type":"text","value":" hook."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This un-customized "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useAppForm"},{"type":"text","value":" hook is identical to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useForm"},{"type":"text","value":", but that will quickly change as we add more options to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createFormHook"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createFormHookContexts, createFormHook } from '@tanstack/solid-form'\n\n// export useFieldContext for use in your custom components\nexport const { fieldContext, formContext, useFieldContext } =\n  createFormHookContexts()\n\nconst { useAppForm } = createFormHook({\n  fieldContext,\n  formContext,\n  // We'll learn more about these options later\n  fieldComponents: {},\n  formComponents: {},\n})\n\nfunction App() {\n  const form = useAppForm(() => ({\n    // Supports all useForm options\n    defaultValues: {\n      firstName: 'John',\n      lastName: 'Doe',\n    },\n  }))\n\n  return <form.Field /> // ...\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"pre-bound-field-components","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pre-bound Field Components"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Once this scaffolding is in place, you can start adding custom field and form components to your form hook."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Note: the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"useFieldContext"},{"type":"text","value":" must be the same one exported from your custom form context"}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useFieldContext } from './form-context.tsx'\n\nexport function TextField(props: { label: string }) {\n  // The `Field` infers that it should have a `value` type of `string`\n  const field = useFieldContext<string>()\n  return (\n    <label>\n      <div>{props.label}</div>\n      <input\n        value={field().state.value}\n        onChange={(e) => field().handleChange(e.target.value)}\n      />\n    </label>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You're then able to register this component with your form hook."}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { TextField } from './text-field.tsx'\n\nconst { useAppForm } = createFormHook({\n  fieldContext,\n  formContext,\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {},\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"And use it in your form:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function App() {\n  const form = useAppForm(() => ({\n    defaultValues: {\n      firstName: 'John',\n      lastName: 'Doe',\n    },\n  }))\n\n  return (\n    // Notice the `AppField` instead of `Field`; `AppField` provides the required context\n    <form.AppField\n      name=\"firstName\"\n      children={(field) => <field.TextField label=\"First Name\" />}\n    />\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This not only allows you to reuse the UI of your shared component, but retains the type-safety you'd expect from TanStack Form: Typo "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"name"},{"type":"text","value":" and get a TypeScript error."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"pre-bound-form-components","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pre-bound Form Components"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"While "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form.AppField"},{"type":"text","value":" solves many of the problems with Field boilerplate and reusability, it doesn't solve the problem of "},{"type":"emphasis","children":[{"type":"text","value":"form"}]},{"type":"text","value":" boilerplate and reusability."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"In particular, being able to share instances of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"form.Subscribe"},{"type":"text","value":" for, say, a reactive form submission button is a common usecase."}]},{"type":"code","value":"function SubscribeButton(props: { label: string }) {\n  const form = useFormContext()\n  return (\n    <form.Subscribe selector={(state) => state.isSubmitting}>\n      {(isSubmitting) => (\n        <button type=\"submit\" disabled={isSubmitting()}>\n          {props.label}\n        </button>\n      )}\n    </form.Subscribe>\n  )\n}\n\nconst { useAppForm, withForm } = createFormHook({\n  fieldComponents: {},\n  formComponents: {\n    SubscribeButton,\n  },\n  fieldContext,\n  formContext,\n})\n\nfunction App() {\n  const form = useAppForm(() => ({\n    defaultValues: {\n      firstName: 'John',\n      lastName: 'Doe',\n    },\n  }))\n\n  return (\n    <form.AppForm>\n      // Notice the `AppForm` component wrapper; `AppForm` provides the required\n      context\n      <form.SubscribeButton label=\"Submit\" />\n    </form.AppForm>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"breaking-big-forms-into-smaller-pieces","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Breaking big forms into smaller pieces"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sometimes forms get very large; it's just how it goes sometimes. While TanStack Form supports large forms well, it's never fun to work with hundreds or thousands of lines of code long files."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To solve this, we support breaking forms into smaller pieces using the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withForm"},{"type":"text","value":" higher-order component."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const { useAppForm, withForm, withFieldGroup } = createFormHook({\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {\n    SubscribeButton,\n  },\n  fieldContext,\n  formContext,\n})\n\nconst ChildForm = withForm({\n  // These values are only used for type-checking, and are not used at runtime\n  // This allows you to `...formOpts` from `formOptions` without needing to redeclare the options\n  defaultValues: {\n    firstName: 'John',\n    lastName: 'Doe',\n  },\n  // Optional, but adds props to the `render` function in addition to `form`\n  props: {\n    // These props are also set as default values for the `render` function\n    title: 'Child Form',\n  },\n  render: function Render(props) {\n    return (\n      <div>\n        <p>{props.title}</p>\n        <props.form.AppField\n          name=\"firstName\"\n          children={(field) => <field.TextField label=\"First Name\" />}\n        />\n        <props.form.AppForm>\n          <props.form.SubscribeButton label=\"Submit\" />\n        </props.form.AppForm>\n      </div>\n    )\n  },\n})\n\nfunction App() {\n  const form = useAppForm(() => ({\n    defaultValues: {\n      firstName: 'John',\n      lastName: 'Doe',\n    },\n  }))\n\n  return <ChildForm form={form} title={'Testing'} />\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"withform-faq","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withForm"},{"type":"text","value":" FAQ"}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why a higher-order component instead of a hook?"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"While hooks are the future of Solid, higher-order components are still a powerful tool for composition. In particular, the API of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withForm"},{"type":"text","value":" enables us to have strong type-safety without requiring users to pass generics."}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"reusing-groups-of-fields-in-multiple-forms","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reusing groups of fields in multiple forms"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Sometimes, a pair of fields are so closely related that it makes sense to group and reuse them — like the password example listed in the "},{"type":"link","href":"./linked-fields.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"linked fields guide"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Instead of repeating this logic across multiple forms, you can utilize the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withFieldGroup"},{"type":"text","value":" higher-order component."}]},{"type":"blockquote","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unlike "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withForm"},{"type":"text","value":", validators cannot be specified and could be any value.\nEnsure that your fields can accept unknown error types."}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rewriting the passwords example using "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"withFieldGroup"},{"type":"text","value":" would look like this:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const { useAppForm, withForm, withFieldGroup } = createFormHook({\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n    ErrorInfo,\n  },\n  formComponents: {\n    SubscribeButton,\n  },\n  fieldContext,\n  formContext,\n})\n\ntype PasswordFields = {\n  password: string\n  confirm_password: string\n}\n\n// These default values are not used at runtime, but the keys are needed for mapping purposes.\n// This allows you to spread `formOptions` without needing to redeclare it.\nconst defaultValues: PasswordFields = {\n  password: '',\n  confirm_password: '',\n}\n\nconst FieldGroupPasswordFields = withFieldGroup({\n  defaultValues,\n  // You may also restrict the group to only use forms that implement this submit meta.\n  // If none is provided, any form with the right defaultValues may use it.\n  // onSubmitMeta: { action: '' }\n\n  // Optional, but adds props to the `render` function in addition to `form`\n  props: {\n    // These props are set as default values for the `render` function\n    title: 'Password',\n  },\n  // Internally, you will have access to a `group` instead of a `form`\n  render: function Render(props) {\n    // access reactive values using the group store\n    const password = useSelector(\n      props.group.store,\n      (state) => state.values.password,\n    )\n    // or the form itself\n    const isSubmitting = useSelector(\n      props.group.form.store,\n      (state) => state.isSubmitting,\n    )\n\n    return (\n      <div>\n        <h2>{props.title}</h2>\n        {/* Groups also have access to Field, Subscribe, Field, AppField and AppForm */}\n        <props.group.AppField name=\"password\">\n          {(field) => <field.TextField label=\"Password\" />}\n        </props.group.AppField>\n        <props.group.AppField\n          name=\"confirm_password\"\n          validators={{\n            onChangeListenTo: ['password'],\n            onChange: ({ value, fieldApi }) => {\n              // The form could be any values, so it is typed as 'unknown'\n              const values: unknown = fieldApi.form.state.values\n              // use the group methods instead\n              if (value !== props.group.getFieldValue('password')) {\n                return 'Passwords do not match'\n              }\n              return undefined\n            },\n          }}\n        >\n          {(field) => (\n            <div>\n              <field.TextField label=\"Confirm Password\" />\n              <field.ErrorInfo />\n            </div>\n          )}\n        </props.group.AppField>\n      </div>\n    )\n  },\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"We can now use these grouped fields in any form that implements the default values:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// You are allowed to extend the group fields as long as the\n// existing properties remain unchanged\ntype Account = PasswordFields & {\n  provider: string\n  username: string\n}\n\n// You may nest the group fields wherever you want\ntype FormValues = {\n  name: string\n  age: number\n  account_data: PasswordFields\n  linked_accounts: Account[]\n}\n\nconst defaultValues: FormValues = {\n  name: '',\n  age: 0,\n  account_data: {\n    password: '',\n    confirm_password: '',\n  },\n  linked_accounts: [\n    {\n      provider: 'TanStack',\n      username: '',\n      password: '',\n      confirm_password: '',\n    },\n  ],\n}\n\nfunction App() {\n  const form = useAppForm(() => ({\n    defaultValues,\n    // If the group didn't specify an `onSubmitMeta` property,\n    // the form may implement any meta it wants.\n    // Otherwise, the meta must be defined and match.\n    onSubmitMeta: { action: '' },\n  }))\n\n  return (\n    <form.AppForm>\n      <FieldGroupPasswordFields\n        form={form}\n        // You must specify where the fields can be found\n        fields=\"account_data\"\n        title=\"Passwords\"\n      />\n      <form.Field name=\"linked_accounts\" mode=\"array\">\n        {(field) =>\n          field().state.value.map((account, i) => (\n            <FieldGroupPasswordFields\n              key={account.provider}\n              form={form}\n              // The fields may be in nested fields\n              fields={`linked_accounts[${i}]`}\n              title={account.provider}\n            />\n          ))\n        }\n      </form.Field>\n    </form.AppForm>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"mapping-field-group-values-to-a-different-field","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mapping field group values to a different field"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"You may want to keep the password fields on the top level of your form, or rename the properties for clarity. You can map field group values\nto their true location by changing the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"field"},{"type":"text","value":" property:"}]},{"type":"callout","kind":"important","title":"Important","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Due to TypeScript limitations, field mapping is only allowed for objects. You can use records or arrays at the top level of a field group, but you will not be able to map the fields."}]}]},{"type":"code","value":"// To have an easier form, you can keep the fields on the top level\ntype FormValues = {\n  name: string\n  age: number\n  password: string\n  confirm_password: string\n}\n\nconst defaultValues: FormValues = {\n  name: '',\n  age: 0,\n  password: '',\n  confirm_password: '',\n}\n\nfunction App() {\n  const form = useAppForm(() => ({\n    defaultValues,\n  }))\n\n  return (\n    <form.AppForm>\n      <FieldGroupPasswordFields\n        form={form}\n        // You can map the fields to their equivalent deep key\n        fields={{\n          password: 'password',\n          confirm_password: 'confirm_password',\n          // or map them to differently named keys entirely\n          // 'password': 'name'\n        }}\n        title=\"Passwords\"\n      />\n    </form.AppForm>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"If you expect your fields to always be at the top level of your form, you can create a quick map\nof your field groups using a helper function:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const defaultValues: PasswordFields = {\n  password: '',\n  confirm_password: '',\n}\n\nconst passwordFields = createFieldMap(defaultValues)\n/* This generates the following map:\n {\n    'password': 'password',\n    'confirm_password': 'confirm_password'\n }\n*/\n\n// Usage:\n<FieldGroupPasswordFields\n  form={form}\n  fields={passwordFields}\n  title=\"Passwords\"\n/>","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"tree-shaking-form-and-field-components","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Tree-shaking form and field components"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"While the above examples are great for getting started, they're not ideal for certain use-cases where you might have hundreds of form and field components.\nIn particular, you may not want to include all of your form and field components in the bundle of every file that uses your form hook."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To solve this, you can mix the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createFormHook"},{"type":"text","value":" TanStack API with the Solid "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"lazy"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Suspense"},{"type":"text","value":" components:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"// src/hooks/form-context.ts\nimport { createFormHookContexts } from '@tanstack/solid-form'\n\nexport const { fieldContext, useFieldContext, formContext, useFormContext } =\n  createFormHookContexts()","lang":"typescript"},{"type":"code","value":"// src/components/text-field.tsx\nimport { useFieldContext } from '../hooks/form-context.tsx'\n\nexport default function TextField(props: { label: string }) {\n  const field = useFieldContext<string>()\n\n  return (\n    <label>\n      <div>{props.label}</div>\n      <input\n        value={field().state.value}\n        onChange={(e) => field().handleChange(e.target.value)}\n      />\n    </label>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"code","value":"// src/hooks/form.ts\nimport { lazy } from 'solid-js'\nimport { createFormHook } from '@tanstack/solid-form'\n\nconst TextField = lazy(() => import('../components/text-fields.tsx'))\n\nconst { useAppForm, withForm } = createFormHook({\n  fieldContext,\n  formContext,\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {},\n})","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"code","value":"// src/App.tsx\nimport { Suspense } from 'solid-js'\nimport { PeoplePage } from './features/people/form.tsx'\n\nexport default function App() {\n  return (\n    <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading...</p>}>\n      <PeoplePage />\n    </Suspense>\n  )\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This will show the Suspense fallback while the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TextField"},{"type":"text","value":" component is being loaded, and then render the form once it's loaded."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"putting-it-all-together","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Putting it all together"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Now that we've covered the basics of creating custom form hooks, let's put it all together in a single example."}]},{"type":"code","value":"// /src/hooks/form.ts, to be used across the entire app\nconst { fieldContext, useFieldContext, formContext, useFormContext } =\n  createFormHookContexts()\n\nfunction TextField(props: { label: string }) {\n  const field = useFieldContext<string>()\n  return (\n    <label>\n      <div>{props.label}</div>\n      <input\n        value={field().state.value}\n        onChange={(e) => field().handleChange(e.target.value)}\n      />\n    </label>\n  )\n}\n\nfunction SubscribeButton(props: { label: string }) {\n  const form = useFormContext()\n  return (\n    <form.Subscribe selector={(state) => state.isSubmitting}>\n      {(isSubmitting) => (\n        <button disabled={isSubmitting()}>{props.label}</button>\n      )}\n    </form.Subscribe>\n  )\n}\n\nconst { useAppForm, withForm, withFieldGroup } = createFormHook({\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {\n    SubscribeButton,\n  },\n  fieldContext,\n  formContext,\n})\n\n// /src/features/people/shared-form.ts, to be used across `people` features\nconst formOpts = formOptions({\n  defaultValues: {\n    firstName: 'John',\n    lastName: 'Doe',\n  },\n})\n\n// /src/features/people/nested-form.ts, to be used in the `people` page\nconst ChildForm = withForm({\n  ...formOpts,\n  // Optional, but adds props to the `render` function outside of `form`\n  props: {\n    title: 'Child Form',\n  },\n  render: (props) => {\n    return (\n      <div>\n        <p>{props.title}</p>\n        <props.form.AppField\n          name=\"firstName\"\n          children={(field) => <field.TextField label=\"First Name\" />}\n        />\n        <props.form.AppForm>\n          <props.form.SubscribeButton label=\"Submit\" />\n        </props.form.AppForm>\n      </div>\n    )\n  },\n})\n\n// /src/features/people/page.ts\nconst Parent = () => {\n  const form = useAppForm(() => ({\n    ...formOpts,\n  }))\n\n  return <ChildForm form={form} title={'Testing'} />\n}","lang":"tsx"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"api-usage-guidance","children":[{"type":"text","value":"API Usage Guidance"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Here's a chart to help you decide what APIs you should be using:"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"image","src":"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/TanStack/form/main/docs/assets/react_form_composability.svg","alt":""}]}],"headings":[{"id":"custom-form-hooks","text":"Custom Form Hooks","level":2},{"id":"pre-bound-field-components","text":"Pre-bound Field Components","level":3},{"id":"pre-bound-form-components","text":"Pre-bound Form Components","level":3},{"id":"breaking-big-forms-into-smaller-pieces","text":"Breaking big forms into smaller pieces","level":2},{"id":"withform-faq","text":"withForm FAQ","level":3},{"id":"reusing-groups-of-fields-in-multiple-forms","text":"Reusing groups of fields in multiple forms","level":2},{"id":"mapping-field-group-values-to-a-different-field","text":"Mapping field group values to a different field","level":3},{"id":"tree-shaking-form-and-field-components","text":"Tree-shaking form and field components","level":2},{"id":"putting-it-all-together","text":"Putting it all together","level":2},{"id":"api-usage-guidance","text":"API Usage Guidance","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nA common criticism of TanStack Form is its verbosity out-of-the-box. While this _can_ be useful for educational purposes - helping enforce understanding our APIs - it's not ideal in production use cases.\n\nAs a result, while `form.Field` enables the most powerful and flexible usage of TanStack Form, we provide APIs that wrap it and make your application code less verbose.\n\n## Custom Form Hooks\n\nThe most powerful way to compose forms is to create custom form hooks. This allows you to create a form hook that is tailored to your application's needs, including pre-bound custom UI components and more.\n\nAt it's most basic, `createFormHook` is a function that takes a `fieldContext` and `formContext` and returns a `useAppForm` hook.\n\n> This un-customized `useAppForm` hook is identical to `useForm`, but that will quickly change as we add more options to `createFormHook`.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createFormHookContexts, createFormHook } from '@tanstack/solid-form'\n\n// export useFieldContext for use in your custom components\nexport const { fieldContext, formContext, useFieldContext } =\n  createFormHookContexts()\n\nconst { useAppForm } = createFormHook({\n  fieldContext,\n  formContext,\n  // We'll learn more about these options later\n  fieldComponents: {},\n  formComponents: {},\n})\n\nfunction App() {\n  const form = useAppForm(() => ({\n    // Supports all useForm options\n    defaultValues: {\n      firstName: 'John',\n      lastName: 'Doe',\n    },\n  }))\n\n  return <form.Field /> // ...\n}\n```\n\n### Pre-bound Field Components\n\nOnce this scaffolding is in place, you can start adding custom field and form components to your form hook.\n\n> Note: the `useFieldContext` must be the same one exported from your custom form context\n\n```tsx\nimport { useFieldContext } from './form-context.tsx'\n\nexport function TextField(props: { label: string }) {\n  // The `Field` infers that it should have a `value` type of `string`\n  const field = useFieldContext<string>()\n  return (\n    <label>\n      <div>{props.label}</div>\n      <input\n        value={field().state.value}\n        onChange={(e) => field().handleChange(e.target.value)}\n      />\n    </label>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nYou're then able to register this component with your form hook.\n\n```tsx\nimport { TextField } from './text-field.tsx'\n\nconst { useAppForm } = createFormHook({\n  fieldContext,\n  formContext,\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {},\n})\n```\n\nAnd use it in your form:\n\n```tsx\nfunction App() {\n  const form = useAppForm(() => ({\n    defaultValues: {\n      firstName: 'John',\n      lastName: 'Doe',\n    },\n  }))\n\n  return (\n    // Notice the `AppField` instead of `Field`; `AppField` provides the required context\n    <form.AppField\n      name=\"firstName\"\n      children={(field) => <field.TextField label=\"First Name\" />}\n    />\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThis not only allows you to reuse the UI of your shared component, but retains the type-safety you'd expect from TanStack Form: Typo `name` and get a TypeScript error.\n\n### Pre-bound Form Components\n\nWhile `form.AppField` solves many of the problems with Field boilerplate and reusability, it doesn't solve the problem of _form_ boilerplate and reusability.\n\nIn particular, being able to share instances of `form.Subscribe` for, say, a reactive form submission button is a common usecase.\n\n```tsx\nfunction SubscribeButton(props: { label: string }) {\n  const form = useFormContext()\n  return (\n    <form.Subscribe selector={(state) => state.isSubmitting}>\n      {(isSubmitting) => (\n        <button type=\"submit\" disabled={isSubmitting()}>\n          {props.label}\n        </button>\n      )}\n    </form.Subscribe>\n  )\n}\n\nconst { useAppForm, withForm } = createFormHook({\n  fieldComponents: {},\n  formComponents: {\n    SubscribeButton,\n  },\n  fieldContext,\n  formContext,\n})\n\nfunction App() {\n  const form = useAppForm(() => ({\n    defaultValues: {\n      firstName: 'John',\n      lastName: 'Doe',\n    },\n  }))\n\n  return (\n    <form.AppForm>\n      // Notice the `AppForm` component wrapper; `AppForm` provides the required\n      context\n      <form.SubscribeButton label=\"Submit\" />\n    </form.AppForm>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n## Breaking big forms into smaller pieces\n\nSometimes forms get very large; it's just how it goes sometimes. While TanStack Form supports large forms well, it's never fun to work with hundreds or thousands of lines of code long files.\n\nTo solve this, we support breaking forms into smaller pieces using the `withForm` higher-order component.\n\n```tsx\nconst { useAppForm, withForm, withFieldGroup } = createFormHook({\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {\n    SubscribeButton,\n  },\n  fieldContext,\n  formContext,\n})\n\nconst ChildForm = withForm({\n  // These values are only used for type-checking, and are not used at runtime\n  // This allows you to `...formOpts` from `formOptions` without needing to redeclare the options\n  defaultValues: {\n    firstName: 'John',\n    lastName: 'Doe',\n  },\n  // Optional, but adds props to the `render` function in addition to `form`\n  props: {\n    // These props are also set as default values for the `render` function\n    title: 'Child Form',\n  },\n  render: function Render(props) {\n    return (\n      <div>\n        <p>{props.title}</p>\n        <props.form.AppField\n          name=\"firstName\"\n          children={(field) => <field.TextField label=\"First Name\" />}\n        />\n        <props.form.AppForm>\n          <props.form.SubscribeButton label=\"Submit\" />\n        </props.form.AppForm>\n      </div>\n    )\n  },\n})\n\nfunction App() {\n  const form = useAppForm(() => ({\n    defaultValues: {\n      firstName: 'John',\n      lastName: 'Doe',\n    },\n  }))\n\n  return <ChildForm form={form} title={'Testing'} />\n}\n```\n\n### `withForm` FAQ\n\n> Why a higher-order component instead of a hook?\n>\n> While hooks are the future of Solid, higher-order components are still a powerful tool for composition. In particular, the API of `withForm` enables us to have strong type-safety without requiring users to pass generics.\n\n## Reusing groups of fields in multiple forms\n\nSometimes, a pair of fields are so closely related that it makes sense to group and reuse them — like the password example listed in the [linked fields guide](./linked-fields.md). Instead of repeating this logic across multiple forms, you can utilize the `withFieldGroup` higher-order component.\n\n> Unlike `withForm`, validators cannot be specified and could be any value.\n> Ensure that your fields can accept unknown error types.\n\nRewriting the passwords example using `withFieldGroup` would look like this:\n\n```tsx\nconst { useAppForm, withForm, withFieldGroup } = createFormHook({\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n    ErrorInfo,\n  },\n  formComponents: {\n    SubscribeButton,\n  },\n  fieldContext,\n  formContext,\n})\n\ntype PasswordFields = {\n  password: string\n  confirm_password: string\n}\n\n// These default values are not used at runtime, but the keys are needed for mapping purposes.\n// This allows you to spread `formOptions` without needing to redeclare it.\nconst defaultValues: PasswordFields = {\n  password: '',\n  confirm_password: '',\n}\n\nconst FieldGroupPasswordFields = withFieldGroup({\n  defaultValues,\n  // You may also restrict the group to only use forms that implement this submit meta.\n  // If none is provided, any form with the right defaultValues may use it.\n  // onSubmitMeta: { action: '' }\n\n  // Optional, but adds props to the `render` function in addition to `form`\n  props: {\n    // These props are set as default values for the `render` function\n    title: 'Password',\n  },\n  // Internally, you will have access to a `group` instead of a `form`\n  render: function Render(props) {\n    // access reactive values using the group store\n    const password = useSelector(\n      props.group.store,\n      (state) => state.values.password,\n    )\n    // or the form itself\n    const isSubmitting = useSelector(\n      props.group.form.store,\n      (state) => state.isSubmitting,\n    )\n\n    return (\n      <div>\n        <h2>{props.title}</h2>\n        {/* Groups also have access to Field, Subscribe, Field, AppField and AppForm */}\n        <props.group.AppField name=\"password\">\n          {(field) => <field.TextField label=\"Password\" />}\n        </props.group.AppField>\n        <props.group.AppField\n          name=\"confirm_password\"\n          validators={{\n            onChangeListenTo: ['password'],\n            onChange: ({ value, fieldApi }) => {\n              // The form could be any values, so it is typed as 'unknown'\n              const values: unknown = fieldApi.form.state.values\n              // use the group methods instead\n              if (value !== props.group.getFieldValue('password')) {\n                return 'Passwords do not match'\n              }\n              return undefined\n            },\n          }}\n        >\n          {(field) => (\n            <div>\n              <field.TextField label=\"Confirm Password\" />\n              <field.ErrorInfo />\n            </div>\n          )}\n        </props.group.AppField>\n      </div>\n    )\n  },\n})\n```\n\nWe can now use these grouped fields in any form that implements the default values:\n\n```tsx\n// You are allowed to extend the group fields as long as the\n// existing properties remain unchanged\ntype Account = PasswordFields & {\n  provider: string\n  username: string\n}\n\n// You may nest the group fields wherever you want\ntype FormValues = {\n  name: string\n  age: number\n  account_data: PasswordFields\n  linked_accounts: Account[]\n}\n\nconst defaultValues: FormValues = {\n  name: '',\n  age: 0,\n  account_data: {\n    password: '',\n    confirm_password: '',\n  },\n  linked_accounts: [\n    {\n      provider: 'TanStack',\n      username: '',\n      password: '',\n      confirm_password: '',\n    },\n  ],\n}\n\nfunction App() {\n  const form = useAppForm(() => ({\n    defaultValues,\n    // If the group didn't specify an `onSubmitMeta` property,\n    // the form may implement any meta it wants.\n    // Otherwise, the meta must be defined and match.\n    onSubmitMeta: { action: '' },\n  }))\n\n  return (\n    <form.AppForm>\n      <FieldGroupPasswordFields\n        form={form}\n        // You must specify where the fields can be found\n        fields=\"account_data\"\n        title=\"Passwords\"\n      />\n      <form.Field name=\"linked_accounts\" mode=\"array\">\n        {(field) =>\n          field().state.value.map((account, i) => (\n            <FieldGroupPasswordFields\n              key={account.provider}\n              form={form}\n              // The fields may be in nested fields\n              fields={`linked_accounts[${i}]`}\n              title={account.provider}\n            />\n          ))\n        }\n      </form.Field>\n    </form.AppForm>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n### Mapping field group values to a different field\n\nYou may want to keep the password fields on the top level of your form, or rename the properties for clarity. You can map field group values\nto their true location by changing the `field` property:\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Due to TypeScript limitations, field mapping is only allowed for objects. You can use records or arrays at the top level of a field group, but you will not be able to map the fields.\n\n```tsx\n// To have an easier form, you can keep the fields on the top level\ntype FormValues = {\n  name: string\n  age: number\n  password: string\n  confirm_password: string\n}\n\nconst defaultValues: FormValues = {\n  name: '',\n  age: 0,\n  password: '',\n  confirm_password: '',\n}\n\nfunction App() {\n  const form = useAppForm(() => ({\n    defaultValues,\n  }))\n\n  return (\n    <form.AppForm>\n      <FieldGroupPasswordFields\n        form={form}\n        // You can map the fields to their equivalent deep key\n        fields={{\n          password: 'password',\n          confirm_password: 'confirm_password',\n          // or map them to differently named keys entirely\n          // 'password': 'name'\n        }}\n        title=\"Passwords\"\n      />\n    </form.AppForm>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nIf you expect your fields to always be at the top level of your form, you can create a quick map\nof your field groups using a helper function:\n\n```tsx\nconst defaultValues: PasswordFields = {\n  password: '',\n  confirm_password: '',\n}\n\nconst passwordFields = createFieldMap(defaultValues)\n/* This generates the following map:\n {\n    'password': 'password',\n    'confirm_password': 'confirm_password'\n }\n*/\n\n// Usage:\n<FieldGroupPasswordFields\n  form={form}\n  fields={passwordFields}\n  title=\"Passwords\"\n/>\n```\n\n## Tree-shaking form and field components\n\nWhile the above examples are great for getting started, they're not ideal for certain use-cases where you might have hundreds of form and field components.\nIn particular, you may not want to include all of your form and field components in the bundle of every file that uses your form hook.\n\nTo solve this, you can mix the `createFormHook` TanStack API with the Solid `lazy` and `Suspense` components:\n\n```typescript\n// src/hooks/form-context.ts\nimport { createFormHookContexts } from '@tanstack/solid-form'\n\nexport const { fieldContext, useFieldContext, formContext, useFormContext } =\n  createFormHookContexts()\n```\n\n```tsx\n// src/components/text-field.tsx\nimport { useFieldContext } from '../hooks/form-context.tsx'\n\nexport default function TextField(props: { label: string }) {\n  const field = useFieldContext<string>()\n\n  return (\n    <label>\n      <div>{props.label}</div>\n      <input\n        value={field().state.value}\n        onChange={(e) => field().handleChange(e.target.value)}\n      />\n    </label>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n```tsx\n// src/hooks/form.ts\nimport { lazy } from 'solid-js'\nimport { createFormHook } from '@tanstack/solid-form'\n\nconst TextField = lazy(() => import('../components/text-fields.tsx'))\n\nconst { useAppForm, withForm } = createFormHook({\n  fieldContext,\n  formContext,\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {},\n})\n```\n\n```tsx\n// src/App.tsx\nimport { Suspense } from 'solid-js'\nimport { PeoplePage } from './features/people/form.tsx'\n\nexport default function App() {\n  return (\n    <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading...</p>}>\n      <PeoplePage />\n    </Suspense>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThis will show the Suspense fallback while the `TextField` component is being loaded, and then render the form once it's loaded.\n\n## Putting it all together\n\nNow that we've covered the basics of creating custom form hooks, let's put it all together in a single example.\n\n```tsx\n// /src/hooks/form.ts, to be used across the entire app\nconst { fieldContext, useFieldContext, formContext, useFormContext } =\n  createFormHookContexts()\n\nfunction TextField(props: { label: string }) {\n  const field = useFieldContext<string>()\n  return (\n    <label>\n      <div>{props.label}</div>\n      <input\n        value={field().state.value}\n        onChange={(e) => field().handleChange(e.target.value)}\n      />\n    </label>\n  )\n}\n\nfunction SubscribeButton(props: { label: string }) {\n  const form = useFormContext()\n  return (\n    <form.Subscribe selector={(state) => state.isSubmitting}>\n      {(isSubmitting) => (\n        <button disabled={isSubmitting()}>{props.label}</button>\n      )}\n    </form.Subscribe>\n  )\n}\n\nconst { useAppForm, withForm, withFieldGroup } = createFormHook({\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {\n    SubscribeButton,\n  },\n  fieldContext,\n  formContext,\n})\n\n// /src/features/people/shared-form.ts, to be used across `people` features\nconst formOpts = formOptions({\n  defaultValues: {\n    firstName: 'John',\n    lastName: 'Doe',\n  },\n})\n\n// /src/features/people/nested-form.ts, to be used in the `people` page\nconst ChildForm = withForm({\n  ...formOpts,\n  // Optional, but adds props to the `render` function outside of `form`\n  props: {\n    title: 'Child Form',\n  },\n  render: (props) => {\n    return (\n      <div>\n        <p>{props.title}</p>\n        <props.form.AppField\n          name=\"firstName\"\n          children={(field) => <field.TextField label=\"First Name\" />}\n        />\n        <props.form.AppForm>\n          <props.form.SubscribeButton label=\"Submit\" />\n        </props.form.AppForm>\n      </div>\n    )\n  },\n})\n\n// /src/features/people/page.ts\nconst Parent = () => {\n  const form = useAppForm(() => ({\n    ...formOpts,\n  }))\n\n  return <ChildForm form={form} title={'Testing'} />\n}\n```\n\n## API Usage Guidance\n\nHere's a chart to help you decide what APIs you should be using:\n\n![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/TanStack/form/main/docs/assets/react_form_composability.svg)\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: form-composition\ntitle: Form Composition\n---\n\nA common criticism of TanStack Form is its verbosity out-of-the-box. While this _can_ be useful for educational purposes - helping enforce understanding our APIs - it's not ideal in production use cases.\n\nAs a result, while `form.Field` enables the most powerful and flexible usage of TanStack Form, we provide APIs that wrap it and make your application code less verbose.\n\n## Custom Form Hooks\n\nThe most powerful way to compose forms is to create custom form hooks. This allows you to create a form hook that is tailored to your application's needs, including pre-bound custom UI components and more.\n\nAt it's most basic, `createFormHook` is a function that takes a `fieldContext` and `formContext` and returns a `useAppForm` hook.\n\n> This un-customized `useAppForm` hook is identical to `useForm`, but that will quickly change as we add more options to `createFormHook`.\n\n```tsx\nimport { createFormHookContexts, createFormHook } from '@tanstack/solid-form'\n\n// export useFieldContext for use in your custom components\nexport const { fieldContext, formContext, useFieldContext } =\n  createFormHookContexts()\n\nconst { useAppForm } = createFormHook({\n  fieldContext,\n  formContext,\n  // We'll learn more about these options later\n  fieldComponents: {},\n  formComponents: {},\n})\n\nfunction App() {\n  const form = useAppForm(() => ({\n    // Supports all useForm options\n    defaultValues: {\n      firstName: 'John',\n      lastName: 'Doe',\n    },\n  }))\n\n  return <form.Field /> // ...\n}\n```\n\n### Pre-bound Field Components\n\nOnce this scaffolding is in place, you can start adding custom field and form components to your form hook.\n\n> Note: the `useFieldContext` must be the same one exported from your custom form context\n\n```tsx\nimport { useFieldContext } from './form-context.tsx'\n\nexport function TextField(props: { label: string }) {\n  // The `Field` infers that it should have a `value` type of `string`\n  const field = useFieldContext<string>()\n  return (\n    <label>\n      <div>{props.label}</div>\n      <input\n        value={field().state.value}\n        onChange={(e) => field().handleChange(e.target.value)}\n      />\n    </label>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nYou're then able to register this component with your form hook.\n\n```tsx\nimport { TextField } from './text-field.tsx'\n\nconst { useAppForm } = createFormHook({\n  fieldContext,\n  formContext,\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {},\n})\n```\n\nAnd use it in your form:\n\n```tsx\nfunction App() {\n  const form = useAppForm(() => ({\n    defaultValues: {\n      firstName: 'John',\n      lastName: 'Doe',\n    },\n  }))\n\n  return (\n    // Notice the `AppField` instead of `Field`; `AppField` provides the required context\n    <form.AppField\n      name=\"firstName\"\n      children={(field) => <field.TextField label=\"First Name\" />}\n    />\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThis not only allows you to reuse the UI of your shared component, but retains the type-safety you'd expect from TanStack Form: Typo `name` and get a TypeScript error.\n\n### Pre-bound Form Components\n\nWhile `form.AppField` solves many of the problems with Field boilerplate and reusability, it doesn't solve the problem of _form_ boilerplate and reusability.\n\nIn particular, being able to share instances of `form.Subscribe` for, say, a reactive form submission button is a common usecase.\n\n```tsx\nfunction SubscribeButton(props: { label: string }) {\n  const form = useFormContext()\n  return (\n    <form.Subscribe selector={(state) => state.isSubmitting}>\n      {(isSubmitting) => (\n        <button type=\"submit\" disabled={isSubmitting()}>\n          {props.label}\n        </button>\n      )}\n    </form.Subscribe>\n  )\n}\n\nconst { useAppForm, withForm } = createFormHook({\n  fieldComponents: {},\n  formComponents: {\n    SubscribeButton,\n  },\n  fieldContext,\n  formContext,\n})\n\nfunction App() {\n  const form = useAppForm(() => ({\n    defaultValues: {\n      firstName: 'John',\n      lastName: 'Doe',\n    },\n  }))\n\n  return (\n    <form.AppForm>\n      // Notice the `AppForm` component wrapper; `AppForm` provides the required\n      context\n      <form.SubscribeButton label=\"Submit\" />\n    </form.AppForm>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n## Breaking big forms into smaller pieces\n\nSometimes forms get very large; it's just how it goes sometimes. While TanStack Form supports large forms well, it's never fun to work with hundreds or thousands of lines of code long files.\n\nTo solve this, we support breaking forms into smaller pieces using the `withForm` higher-order component.\n\n```tsx\nconst { useAppForm, withForm, withFieldGroup } = createFormHook({\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {\n    SubscribeButton,\n  },\n  fieldContext,\n  formContext,\n})\n\nconst ChildForm = withForm({\n  // These values are only used for type-checking, and are not used at runtime\n  // This allows you to `...formOpts` from `formOptions` without needing to redeclare the options\n  defaultValues: {\n    firstName: 'John',\n    lastName: 'Doe',\n  },\n  // Optional, but adds props to the `render` function in addition to `form`\n  props: {\n    // These props are also set as default values for the `render` function\n    title: 'Child Form',\n  },\n  render: function Render(props) {\n    return (\n      <div>\n        <p>{props.title}</p>\n        <props.form.AppField\n          name=\"firstName\"\n          children={(field) => <field.TextField label=\"First Name\" />}\n        />\n        <props.form.AppForm>\n          <props.form.SubscribeButton label=\"Submit\" />\n        </props.form.AppForm>\n      </div>\n    )\n  },\n})\n\nfunction App() {\n  const form = useAppForm(() => ({\n    defaultValues: {\n      firstName: 'John',\n      lastName: 'Doe',\n    },\n  }))\n\n  return <ChildForm form={form} title={'Testing'} />\n}\n```\n\n### `withForm` FAQ\n\n> Why a higher-order component instead of a hook?\n>\n> While hooks are the future of Solid, higher-order components are still a powerful tool for composition. In particular, the API of `withForm` enables us to have strong type-safety without requiring users to pass generics.\n\n## Reusing groups of fields in multiple forms\n\nSometimes, a pair of fields are so closely related that it makes sense to group and reuse them — like the password example listed in the [linked fields guide](./linked-fields.md). Instead of repeating this logic across multiple forms, you can utilize the `withFieldGroup` higher-order component.\n\n> Unlike `withForm`, validators cannot be specified and could be any value.\n> Ensure that your fields can accept unknown error types.\n\nRewriting the passwords example using `withFieldGroup` would look like this:\n\n```tsx\nconst { useAppForm, withForm, withFieldGroup } = createFormHook({\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n    ErrorInfo,\n  },\n  formComponents: {\n    SubscribeButton,\n  },\n  fieldContext,\n  formContext,\n})\n\ntype PasswordFields = {\n  password: string\n  confirm_password: string\n}\n\n// These default values are not used at runtime, but the keys are needed for mapping purposes.\n// This allows you to spread `formOptions` without needing to redeclare it.\nconst defaultValues: PasswordFields = {\n  password: '',\n  confirm_password: '',\n}\n\nconst FieldGroupPasswordFields = withFieldGroup({\n  defaultValues,\n  // You may also restrict the group to only use forms that implement this submit meta.\n  // If none is provided, any form with the right defaultValues may use it.\n  // onSubmitMeta: { action: '' }\n\n  // Optional, but adds props to the `render` function in addition to `form`\n  props: {\n    // These props are set as default values for the `render` function\n    title: 'Password',\n  },\n  // Internally, you will have access to a `group` instead of a `form`\n  render: function Render(props) {\n    // access reactive values using the group store\n    const password = useSelector(\n      props.group.store,\n      (state) => state.values.password,\n    )\n    // or the form itself\n    const isSubmitting = useSelector(\n      props.group.form.store,\n      (state) => state.isSubmitting,\n    )\n\n    return (\n      <div>\n        <h2>{props.title}</h2>\n        {/* Groups also have access to Field, Subscribe, Field, AppField and AppForm */}\n        <props.group.AppField name=\"password\">\n          {(field) => <field.TextField label=\"Password\" />}\n        </props.group.AppField>\n        <props.group.AppField\n          name=\"confirm_password\"\n          validators={{\n            onChangeListenTo: ['password'],\n            onChange: ({ value, fieldApi }) => {\n              // The form could be any values, so it is typed as 'unknown'\n              const values: unknown = fieldApi.form.state.values\n              // use the group methods instead\n              if (value !== props.group.getFieldValue('password')) {\n                return 'Passwords do not match'\n              }\n              return undefined\n            },\n          }}\n        >\n          {(field) => (\n            <div>\n              <field.TextField label=\"Confirm Password\" />\n              <field.ErrorInfo />\n            </div>\n          )}\n        </props.group.AppField>\n      </div>\n    )\n  },\n})\n```\n\nWe can now use these grouped fields in any form that implements the default values:\n\n```tsx\n// You are allowed to extend the group fields as long as the\n// existing properties remain unchanged\ntype Account = PasswordFields & {\n  provider: string\n  username: string\n}\n\n// You may nest the group fields wherever you want\ntype FormValues = {\n  name: string\n  age: number\n  account_data: PasswordFields\n  linked_accounts: Account[]\n}\n\nconst defaultValues: FormValues = {\n  name: '',\n  age: 0,\n  account_data: {\n    password: '',\n    confirm_password: '',\n  },\n  linked_accounts: [\n    {\n      provider: 'TanStack',\n      username: '',\n      password: '',\n      confirm_password: '',\n    },\n  ],\n}\n\nfunction App() {\n  const form = useAppForm(() => ({\n    defaultValues,\n    // If the group didn't specify an `onSubmitMeta` property,\n    // the form may implement any meta it wants.\n    // Otherwise, the meta must be defined and match.\n    onSubmitMeta: { action: '' },\n  }))\n\n  return (\n    <form.AppForm>\n      <FieldGroupPasswordFields\n        form={form}\n        // You must specify where the fields can be found\n        fields=\"account_data\"\n        title=\"Passwords\"\n      />\n      <form.Field name=\"linked_accounts\" mode=\"array\">\n        {(field) =>\n          field().state.value.map((account, i) => (\n            <FieldGroupPasswordFields\n              key={account.provider}\n              form={form}\n              // The fields may be in nested fields\n              fields={`linked_accounts[${i}]`}\n              title={account.provider}\n            />\n          ))\n        }\n      </form.Field>\n    </form.AppForm>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n### Mapping field group values to a different field\n\nYou may want to keep the password fields on the top level of your form, or rename the properties for clarity. You can map field group values\nto their true location by changing the `field` property:\n\n> [!IMPORTANT]\n> Due to TypeScript limitations, field mapping is only allowed for objects. You can use records or arrays at the top level of a field group, but you will not be able to map the fields.\n\n```tsx\n// To have an easier form, you can keep the fields on the top level\ntype FormValues = {\n  name: string\n  age: number\n  password: string\n  confirm_password: string\n}\n\nconst defaultValues: FormValues = {\n  name: '',\n  age: 0,\n  password: '',\n  confirm_password: '',\n}\n\nfunction App() {\n  const form = useAppForm(() => ({\n    defaultValues,\n  }))\n\n  return (\n    <form.AppForm>\n      <FieldGroupPasswordFields\n        form={form}\n        // You can map the fields to their equivalent deep key\n        fields={{\n          password: 'password',\n          confirm_password: 'confirm_password',\n          // or map them to differently named keys entirely\n          // 'password': 'name'\n        }}\n        title=\"Passwords\"\n      />\n    </form.AppForm>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nIf you expect your fields to always be at the top level of your form, you can create a quick map\nof your field groups using a helper function:\n\n```tsx\nconst defaultValues: PasswordFields = {\n  password: '',\n  confirm_password: '',\n}\n\nconst passwordFields = createFieldMap(defaultValues)\n/* This generates the following map:\n {\n    'password': 'password',\n    'confirm_password': 'confirm_password'\n }\n*/\n\n// Usage:\n<FieldGroupPasswordFields\n  form={form}\n  fields={passwordFields}\n  title=\"Passwords\"\n/>\n```\n\n## Tree-shaking form and field components\n\nWhile the above examples are great for getting started, they're not ideal for certain use-cases where you might have hundreds of form and field components.\nIn particular, you may not want to include all of your form and field components in the bundle of every file that uses your form hook.\n\nTo solve this, you can mix the `createFormHook` TanStack API with the Solid `lazy` and `Suspense` components:\n\n```typescript\n// src/hooks/form-context.ts\nimport { createFormHookContexts } from '@tanstack/solid-form'\n\nexport const { fieldContext, useFieldContext, formContext, useFormContext } =\n  createFormHookContexts()\n```\n\n```tsx\n// src/components/text-field.tsx\nimport { useFieldContext } from '../hooks/form-context.tsx'\n\nexport default function TextField(props: { label: string }) {\n  const field = useFieldContext<string>()\n\n  return (\n    <label>\n      <div>{props.label}</div>\n      <input\n        value={field().state.value}\n        onChange={(e) => field().handleChange(e.target.value)}\n      />\n    </label>\n  )\n}\n```\n\n```tsx\n// src/hooks/form.ts\nimport { lazy } from 'solid-js'\nimport { createFormHook } from '@tanstack/solid-form'\n\nconst TextField = lazy(() => import('../components/text-fields.tsx'))\n\nconst { useAppForm, withForm } = createFormHook({\n  fieldContext,\n  formContext,\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {},\n})\n```\n\n```tsx\n// src/App.tsx\nimport { Suspense } from 'solid-js'\nimport { PeoplePage } from './features/people/form.tsx'\n\nexport default function App() {\n  return (\n    <Suspense fallback={<p>Loading...</p>}>\n      <PeoplePage />\n    </Suspense>\n  )\n}\n```\n\nThis will show the Suspense fallback while the `TextField` component is being loaded, and then render the form once it's loaded.\n\n## Putting it all together\n\nNow that we've covered the basics of creating custom form hooks, let's put it all together in a single example.\n\n```tsx\n// /src/hooks/form.ts, to be used across the entire app\nconst { fieldContext, useFieldContext, formContext, useFormContext } =\n  createFormHookContexts()\n\nfunction TextField(props: { label: string }) {\n  const field = useFieldContext<string>()\n  return (\n    <label>\n      <div>{props.label}</div>\n      <input\n        value={field().state.value}\n        onChange={(e) => field().handleChange(e.target.value)}\n      />\n    </label>\n  )\n}\n\nfunction SubscribeButton(props: { label: string }) {\n  const form = useFormContext()\n  return (\n    <form.Subscribe selector={(state) => state.isSubmitting}>\n      {(isSubmitting) => (\n        <button disabled={isSubmitting()}>{props.label}</button>\n      )}\n    </form.Subscribe>\n  )\n}\n\nconst { useAppForm, withForm, withFieldGroup } = createFormHook({\n  fieldComponents: {\n    TextField,\n  },\n  formComponents: {\n    SubscribeButton,\n  },\n  fieldContext,\n  formContext,\n})\n\n// /src/features/people/shared-form.ts, to be used across `people` features\nconst formOpts = formOptions({\n  defaultValues: {\n    firstName: 'John',\n    lastName: 'Doe',\n  },\n})\n\n// /src/features/people/nested-form.ts, to be used in the `people` page\nconst ChildForm = withForm({\n  ...formOpts,\n  // Optional, but adds props to the `render` function outside of `form`\n  props: {\n    title: 'Child Form',\n  },\n  render: (props) => {\n    return (\n      <div>\n        <p>{props.title}</p>\n        <props.form.AppField\n          name=\"firstName\"\n          children={(field) => <field.TextField label=\"First Name\" />}\n        />\n        <props.form.AppForm>\n          <props.form.SubscribeButton label=\"Submit\" />\n        </props.form.AppForm>\n      </div>\n    )\n  },\n})\n\n// /src/features/people/page.ts\nconst Parent = () => {\n  const form = useAppForm(() => ({\n    ...formOpts,\n  }))\n\n  return <ChildForm form={form} title={'Testing'} />\n}\n```\n\n## API Usage Guidance\n\nHere's a chart to help you decide what APIs you should be using:\n\n![](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/TanStack/form/main/docs/assets/react_form_composability.svg)\n"}
